  <p class="lead-text">
    Mississippi doesn't have a state law requiring every homeowner to carry flood insurance. But depending on where your home sits and how it's financed, your lender may require it, and even when it's optional, it may be one of the most important policies you can buy.
  </p>

  <h2>1. When Flood Insurance Is Required</h2>
  <p>
    Flood insurance becomes mandatory when <strong>both</strong> of these are true:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Your home is in a FEMA-designated <strong>Special Flood Hazard Area</strong> (high-risk zones, which typically start with the letter A or V on flood maps), <strong>and</strong></li>
    <li>Your mortgage comes from a federally regulated or federally insured lender, or is backed by programs like FHA, VA, USDA, Fannie Mae, or Freddie Mac.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    In practice, that covers most mortgages. Lenders can also choose to require flood insurance for homes outside high-risk zones, and they'll often require it for the life of the loan.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Your Homeowners Policy Does Not Cover Flood</h2>
  <p>
    This is the single most common misunderstanding we see. Standard homeowners, renters, and most commercial property policies <strong>exclude flood damage</strong>, meaning water that rises from the outside, such as overflowing creeks and rivers, flash flooding, or heavy rain that pools and enters your home.
  </p>
  <div class="callout-box">
    <h3>⚠️ Flood vs. Water Damage</h3>
    <p>A burst pipe or a leaking water heater is typically covered by homeowners insurance. Water that enters from outside because of rising surface water is flood and requires a separate flood policy. Sewer and drain backup is usually a third category, covered only by an optional endorsement.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>3. Flood Risk Is Real in North Mississippi</h2>
  <p>
    North Mississippi is crisscrossed by rivers, creeks, and reservoirs, and intense spring and fall storms can drop several inches of rain in a short time. Low-lying neighborhoods, homes near creeks and bottomland, and properties with poor drainage can flood even when they're nowhere near a mapped high-risk zone.
  </p>
  <p>
    According to FEMA, <strong>more than 20% of National Flood Insurance Program claims come from properties outside high-risk flood zones</strong>. The good news: policies for homes in moderate- and low-risk areas are often much less expensive.
  </p>

  <h2>4. NFIP vs. Private Flood Insurance</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th></th><th>NFIP</th><th>Private Flood</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Building limit</strong></td><td>Up to $250,000</td><td>Often higher, varies by carrier</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Contents limit</strong></td><td>Up to $100,000</td><td>Often higher, varies by carrier</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Waiting period</strong></td><td>Generally 30 days</td><td>Often shorter</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Extras</strong></td><td>Limited basement coverage, no living expenses</td><td>Some offer additional living expenses and broader coverage</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Availability</strong></td><td>Available in participating communities</td><td>Depends on the property's risk</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Neither option is always better. For many homes, NFIP is the right fit. For higher-value homes, or when a private carrier prices the risk more favorably, private flood can offer more coverage for a similar or lower price. As an independent agency, we can quote both.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Don't Wait: The 30-Day Waiting Period</h2>
  <p>
    NFIP flood policies generally don't take effect until <strong>30 days after purchase</strong>. There are limited exceptions, such as when coverage is bought in connection with closing on a mortgage. That means you can't buy a policy when a storm shows up in the forecast and expect it to cover that storm.
  </p>

  <h2>6. "Won't FEMA Help Me Anyway?"</h2>
  <p>
    Federal disaster assistance is only available when a disaster is officially declared, and grants are typically limited and meant to make a home safe and livable, not to fully rebuild it. Disaster loans must be repaid with interest. A flood insurance policy pays claims whether or not a federal disaster is declared.
  </p>

  <h2>7. How to Check Your Flood Zone</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Look up your address on FEMA's Flood Map Service Center at <strong>msc.fema.gov</strong>.</li>
    <li>Check your mortgage closing documents for a flood zone determination.</li>
    <li>Or just call us at (662) 638-3777. We'll look it up and explain what it means for your coverage and price.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="tip-box">
    <h3>✅ Renters Need Flood Coverage Too</h3>
    <p>Your landlord's flood policy (if they have one) covers the building, not your belongings. Renters can buy contents-only flood coverage, which is often very affordable.</p>
  </div>
